s about 1.046 × the forecast.\n- Candidate 5 orders about 1.05 × forecast, so on average it matches true demand.\n- Candidate 4 orders 1.0 ×, below true demand, so shortages build up (8–10 lost per unit short).\n- Candidate 2 orders about 1.145 ×, roughly 8 units too many per product per cycle. The surplus builds into stock that rarely expires but keeps paying holding, and what's left at the end is wasted. By my estimate, the extra purchases (about 600 for product 0 and 360 for product 1) plus holding (about 390 and 790) cost more than the roughly 550 per product it saves on shortages.\n- Among the daily-order plans, candidate 6 (1.0 ×) and candidate 3 (0.9 ×) order too little, and candidate 0 (1.2 ×) orders too much. Candidate 1 (1.1 ×) is the closest, but it still pays the full daily fees.\n\n**Randomness and capacity.** Ordering every 2 days pools demand across two days, which reduces the relative swing in each cycle. The 292-unit warehouse limit is never close to binding for candidate 5: the busiest morning is about 190 units on day 7.\n\nCandidate 5 combines the better ordering frequency with order sizes that match true average demand."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3784, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3784}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T08:14:12.375Z", "request_id": "req_011CgBG1fZN13vZvUvJBZKW4"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01BFhEuQjkvxhujuJ6MW86Gn", "name": "St